#### Project-local xschem configuration for gf180-bandgap (issue #18).
####
#### xschem's *default* library-path bootstrap (system xschemrc) already adds
#### the generic devices/ library, but any local *user*-level
#### ~/.xschem/xschemrc on a given machine can silently override
#### XSCHEM_LIBRARY_PATH with unrelated, possibly-stale content (observed on
#### the machine this issue audited -- a leftover dev-machine dotfile pointed
#### at directories that no longer exist, which broke `l_s_d(): Symbol not
#### found` for every generic device symbol). Resetting it explicitly here,
#### in a *project-local* rc file, makes this repo's xschem invocations
#### reproducible regardless of whatever is (or isn't) in any given
#### machine's ~/.xschem/xschemrc.
####
#### Load this explicitly and unambiguously (independent of cwd) with:
####   xschem --rcfile design/xschemrc ...
#### (relying on xschem's cwd-relative "./xschemrc" auto-discovery is NOT
#### sufficient here, since that only fires when xschem's cwd happens to be
#### this directory -- see docs/environment-setup.md.)

set XSCHEM_LIBRARY_PATH {}
append XSCHEM_LIBRARY_PATH :${XSCHEM_SHAREDIR}/xschem_library/devices

#### PDK_ROOT / PDK: pass through from the shell environment (see
#### docs/environment-setup.md for the env convention). Not required for
#### netlisting the smoke-test schematic itself -- design/smoke_test.sch
#### references the PDK models indirectly via a generated
#### sim/smoke_test/pdk_include.spice, not via these Tcl variables -- but
#### set for convenience/consistency with the wider xschem+PDK ecosystem
#### convention (see tutorial_xschem_libraries.html in the xschem docs).
if {[info exists env(PDK_ROOT)]} {
  set PDK_ROOT $env(PDK_ROOT)
}
if {[info exists env(PDK)]} {
  set PDK $env(PDK)
}
